e. legal services and/or legal aid; and
f. repatriation and social reintegration services.

Article 19
Health services as referred to in Article 16 letter c shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus with authority over health affairs in the form of:
a. medical assistance to victims;
b. treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
c. implementation of visum et repertum for investigation purposes;
d. provision of services in accordance with SOP to female victims of violence; and
e. referral of health services in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 4
Every female victim of violence has the right to:
a. obtain protection;
b. determine and make her own decisions;
c. obtain information;
d. obtain complaint-handling services;
e. obtain assistance;
f. obtain continuous services up to the rehabilitation stage; and
g. obtain confidential services, whether from individuals, groups, or institutions, whether governmental or non-governmental.

CHAPTER V
OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES
Article 30
Protection of Women Victims of Violence
The Regional Government is obligated to:
a. guarantee the implementation of the fulfillment of women's rights and the provision of assurance of safety from acts of violence, and efforts of exploitation against women in the region;
b. establish policies, programs, activities for the protection of women and guarantees for victim handling to eliminate all forms of discrimination and acts of violence against women in the region;
c. provide guidance and supervision in efforts to protect women carried out by Regional Apparatus handling the Protection and Empowerment of Women;
d. endeavor to establish women's protection institutions;
e. implement the established policies, programs and activities for the protection of women; and
f. conduct socialization of laws and regulations related to the protection of women victims of violence and discrimination;

Article 18
(1) Assistance services as referred to in Article 16 letter b include:
a. assistance to victims during the examination process and health recovery;
b. assistance to victims during the medical examination process for legal purposes;
c. assistance to victims during the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
d. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
e. maintaining the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all interested parties, including reporting by mass media;
f. coordinating with other assistants; and
g. providing rehabilitation.
(2) Assistance Services as referred to in paragraph (1) are services, continuous handling up to the stage referred to, consisting of activities and actions carried out by assistants originating from individuals or representatives of institutions that have expertise in providing assistance to victims to conduct counseling, therapy, and advocacy for the strengthening and self-recovery of victims of violence.

Article 31
The Regional Government is responsible for:
a. implementing activities to ensure the fulfillment of women's rights;
b. providing facilities and infrastructure for the protection of women;
c. applying minimum service standards in women's protection services and the applicable standard operating procedures for repatriation and social reintegration;
d. providing implementing officers and functional officers at the UPTD

Article 14
(1) Prevention of the occurrence of acts of violence against women as referred to in Article 13, shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus that oversee affairs of:
a. health;
b. education;
c. manpower;
d. socio-political;
e. protection and empowerment of women; and
f. peace and order;
(2) Prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan with the Regional Apparatus that oversees Protection and Empowerment of Women.

Article 13
(1) The Regional Government undertakes integrated efforts to prevent violence against women in the Region.
(2) Integrated efforts to prevent violence against women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be coordinated by the Regional Apparatus responsible for the protection and empowerment of women.
(3) Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be implemented by:
a. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns that may involve community organizations, universities, professionals as well as customary heads and/or community leaders;
b. conducting socialization regarding laws and regulations related to the protection of the rights of women victims of violence;
c. establishing an integrated violence prevention system;
d. providing ease of access to obtain civil rights, education, health, employment, training and funding, welfare improvement and social services;
e. providing education regarding women's rights for the community;
f. fostering and developing community participation and concern for the protection of women from acts of violence;
g. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system; and
h. establishing a cooperation network in efforts to prevent acts of violence with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women.

CHAPTER X
ADMINISTRATIVE SANCTIONS
Article 40
(1) Any person, institution, or entity that deliberately fails to provide protection to women, thereby causing acts of violence against women to occur, allows acts of violence to occur, and/or fails to report to the relevant agency may be subject to administrative sanctions.
(2) Business Entities or non-governmental organizations (NGOs) are obligated to provide Protection to Women and against acts of Violence in their work environment.
(3) Business Entities or non-governmental organizations (NGOs) are obligated to report acts of violence in their work environment to the relevant agency.
(4) Violations as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), and paragraph (3) may be subject to administrative sanctions in the form of:
   a. verbal warning;
   b. written warning;
   c. temporary suspension of activities;
   d. termination of cooperation;
   e. revocation of the certificate of registration;
   f. revocation of the operational permit;
   g. sealing;
   h. withdrawal of facilities; and/or
   i. an administrative fine of at least Rp.10,000,000,- (Ten Million Rupiah) and at most Rp.50,000,000,- (Fifty Million Rupiah).
(5) Provisions regarding the procedures for implementing administrative sanctions as referred to in paragraph (4) are regulated in the Regent's Regulation.

Article 11
(1) Sexual Violence as referred to in Article 8 letter c is caused by:
a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. sexual abuse;
c. coercion of sexual relations in an unnatural or unwanted manner;
d. coercion of sexual relations with others and/or exploitation of women for commercial purposes and other specific purposes.
(2) Coercion of sexual relations as referred to in Article 11 paragraph (1) letter c is excluded for persons who have mental disorders or sexual disorders within marital relations.

